    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/esd-3-245-2012",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Various studies have shown the importance of Earth System feedbacks in the climate system and the necessity of including these in models used for making climate change projections. The HadGEM2 family of Met Office Unified Model configurations combines model components which facilitate the representation of many different processes within the climate system, including atmosphere, ocean and sea ice, and Earth System components including the terrestrial and oceanic carbon cycle and tropospheric chemistry. We examine the climatology of the Asian summer monsoon in present-day simulations and in idealised climate change experiments. Members of the HadGEM2 family are used, with a common physical framework (one of which includes tropospheric chemistry and an interactive terrestrial and oceanic carbon cycle), to investigate whether such components affect the way in which the monsoon changes. We focus particularly on the role of interactive vegetation in the simulations from these model configurations. Using an atmosphere-only HadGEM2 configuration, we investigate how the changes in land cover which result from the interaction between the dynamic vegetation and the model systematic rainfall biases affect the Asian summer monsoon, both in the present-day and in future climate projections. We demonstrate that the response of the dynamic vegetation to biases in regional climate, such as lack of rainfall over tropical dust-producing regions, can affect both the present-day simulation and the response to climate change forcing scenarios.",
    "field": "Earth System modeling",
    "problem": "Including dynamic vegetation in climate models is challenging because its response to systematic model rainfall biases can create feedbacks, such as increased dust loading, that amplify errors in simulations and future projections of the South Asian monsoon.",
    "method": "This study uses members of the HadGEM2 model family and sensitivity experiments to investigate how interactive vegetation and associated feedbacks, such as changes in dust, influence the simulation of the present-day and future South Asian summer monsoon.",
    "task": "The task is to investigate the impact of dynamic vegetation on the present-day simulation and future projections of the South Asian summer monsoon using the HadGEM2 family of climate models.",
